the prompt, the views and the click all say the pronoun now.
this is where to pick it up.

read `todo/idea` for the want, `todo/impl` for what was decided and what
it did not take, `todo/frame` for the row a pronoun makes possible. they
split at `todo/impl:/a shell of our own/` — the pronoun is impl, the
screen is frame. `todo/memory` is m's own ranking, `todo/address` the
decision that tmux hands over the word under the pointer, so the grid
was never worth owning.

the pronoun landed, docs with it, and the remap
below is written. what is left is the open list at the foot, and
`todo/frame` — the screen a row makes possible.

landed

fm opens on cur when argv is empty: a file is a row and not a place, so
its directory is where fm stands and its name is what it lands on, which
is the pos map's own answer for a directory seen before. it writes cur
after every ls, temp then rename, and only while the pane has the eye —
without that a background fm takes the shell's this on its next redraw,
and a split resizes every pane, so every fm redraws. the eye is a key
now: KFocus and KBlur are CSI I and O (`tty/term.go:/func csiKey/`),
asked for by t.Focus() and lent to a child by Suspend, forwarded by
`tmux/conf:/focus-events/`. the eye coming back re-says the row —
another pane spoke while we were away. the log stays c's alone.

rename, create and paste land on what they made, so cur follows for
nothing; `fm/4-main/21-catch.go` answers whether there was an error,
which is how a verb knows it made something. ed says the address on
save, with the line, so e bare reopens where the work is. left click is
p -c: the word becomes cur and nothing runs, and the click that says it
is the same click that raises the pane.

@cur is not set by fm or ed, only by c: the status line shows what was
said out loud, not every row a cursor passes. one fork per keypress is
the reason; if the status should follow the cursor, that is the cost to
take on.

fm's g and ; are finds now: they land the cursor and act on nothing, and
x is free because enter already opens. a content hit keeps its line, so
e opens there — `fm/cheatsheet:/f and s select/`, which is where the
remap has since put both letters. it needed none of the order above:
fm's cur is the row, and the row is what a find lands on.

c and m are `sh/c` and `sh/m`. bare, c prints cur's path; with text it
resolves p's way and stores the address, appending the log only when the
address changes. m is here then the log newest first, so m 0 is cur
whole and back is c `{m 1}. cur is $home/.config/sys/cur/@<window>, the
log is one for the machine, and @cur is set beside it. both asks name
the pane, so a background one writes its own window's cur and not the
session's.

o, e, p and a take their noun from c, and a verb hands the next one the
address whole — `{m 0}, not the path — so p's line survives into e. e
still takes +n from a caller that has one, until fm stops sending it. p
lost the resolver and the -a absolutise, gained -c (select and stop, for
a click not yet bound), and names the pane to c so a clicked word writes
its own window and not the session's. a asks c for its directory. c tries
the whole argument as a name before stripping it — a verb's argument is
exact and may end in a bracket; only the screen's leavings need the
strip.

fzo, fze and fzs are gone. at a prompt it is o `{f | fzy}, and
e `{s -n pat | fzy} in one shot: c takes rg's path:line:text whole and
e reads the line back out of cur, so the split fzs did by hand — and
fm's s still does in Go (`fm/3-actions/12-revealaddr.go`) — now lives
in c alone. or keep the middle:
c `{s -n pat | fzy}, then e, o, a and git add `{c} on the one find.
quoting is not needed either way — `sh/c:/name=$"*/` joins its
arguments back into the name a space broke.

the remap, landed

    6b       the rule, so it is not twelve taste calls: a letter that is
             a shell verb means that verb in fm — unless the row already
             does the verb's work. only c and p qualify: the cursor is
             cur (`fm/4-main/27-say.go`), so c has nothing to
             say out loud, and enter dispatches, so p has nothing to
             plumb. f s m o have work in fm, so they cannot drift. only
             lowercase can collide at all — C F M G V Y Z I N have no
             twin on $PATH and never have to move.

    key | was             | becomes
    f   | lint check      | find by name       → cur
    s   | shell (detach)  | find by content    → cur
    m   | clone           | find by memory     → cur
    o   | outline         | duplicate — o on cur, a pane beside you
    ;   | find by content | shell here
    b   | duplicate       | bookmark — say the row out loud
    c   | copy            | copy, kept
    x   | free            | cut
    p   | paste           | paste, kept
    =   | free            | clone
    +   | free            | clone dir
    g   | find by name    | first file — G is already last
    l   | link            | link, unchanged
    z   | zoxide          | zoxide, unchanged
    t   | tag             | tag, unchanged
    F   | format          | format, unchanged
    free: C L M P S T Z

             b is the hole the pronoun left. fm writes cur on every row
             and never touches the log, so nothing you only browsed is
             findable by m. b execs c on the row — the one thing that
             appends — and after an s hit the row still carries its
             line, so it remembers path:line the way ed's save does. b
             puts in, m gets back. t is the other bookmark and keeps its
             key: a tag is a place you can browse and it outlives
             everything; b is an address that ages down the log.

             f s and m are one action and not three: pick, fzy, split
             path:line, reveal. s prompts for a pattern because rg needs
             one; f and m do not, fzy filters the whole list. f yields a
             path, s and m yield addresses and share the split.
             `todo/memory:/by having been there/` is why m ranks beside
             the other two.

             check, outline and duplicate leave the keyboard as whole
             words wanting a noun, and cur is the noun — so lint and
             outline want `*=`{c $*}`, the fold o, e, p and a already
             took, or they lose their noun the moment they lose their
             key. check goes because it was never pressed: the linter
             is folded into what already runs. format keeps F on the
             same argument turned around — uppercase cannot collide, so
             a key that is still pressed costs nothing to keep. link
             keeps l for the same reason. Z goes with nothing in its
             place: f and m find, z jumps, and goto was neither.

             one relearning, not two: four letters change meaning
             (f s m o), three abilities change key (shell, cut, clone),
             one is new (b), two are dropped (goto, and check as a key).

             what the writing turned up: Z took three files with it —
             goto was complete's only caller, and complete was expand's
             and commonprefix's, so fm loses path completion at a
             prompt and nothing else. o on a file row is enter exactly:
             both hand cur to o, and only a directory row tells them
             apart — enter cds, o opens a pane beside. s and m share
             one split (`fm/3-actions/12-revealaddr.go`) and f stays
             out of it, because a bare path is free to carry a colon.
             lint holds -f aside before it asks c, or the flag resolves
             as text; outline turns a file into its directory, the turn
             a already takes; check took no fold — its noun is a tree
             and it defaults to the whole one.

             the log fills faster than b alone. e, o and F from fm go
             out through their scripts, and a script asks c, so a
             verb's argument is a select there too
             (`todo/idea:/a verb's argument is a select/`). b is for
             the row you never handed to anything.

    drift    what is left of it, and why each stays:
             c p    fm means copy and paste, the prompt means cur and
                    plumb. the exception above — neither verb has work
                    in fm, so nothing is being said twice.
             t      sh/t is unwritten, so it collides on paper only. the
                    day the second noun lands, tag moves to T, free for
                    it.
             z      agrees today — zoxide's own shell function is z. if
                    m ever absorbs it the letter frees and the question
                    comes back.
             ed     exempt by kind and not by letter: c change, p paste,
                    s save, f find char, m match bracket, o open below,
                    b word back. the rule is about a view whose nouns
                    are files; ed's nouns are text, and its table is
                    vi's, older than any of this.

open

    zoxide   z and m are the same idea — rank by having been there, m
             over addresses and zoxide over directories. it stopped
             blocking the remap when clone took = and +, so z keeps its
             key until m has been lived with. if the log covers a
             directory you only cd'd into, zoxide is m and z frees.

    key :    reads as ex and README rules it out. unchosen.
    title    ed's status line is at the foot, fm's header at the head.
             with no frame title theirs is the only title there is, so
             they should agree.
    history  tty.ReadLine has none. a slice and two keys.
    t        the second noun, deferred by `todo/idea:/two-noun verbs/`.
             a second noun and not a plural one — that is many, below.

    many     the plural noun is not cur, and it exists twice already:
             fm's sel, the set the space bar fills, and clip, the list
             copy and cut leave — `fm/4-main/20-targets.go` is the
             fallback, selection else cursor. the finders are plural
             too, and fzy is what makes them one, so git add
             `{f '\.go$'} wants no pronoun at all.
             cur stays singular, and not for want of mechanism: a file
             holds lines and both shells splat a capture into argv, so
             git add `{c} would survive three of them. it is that every
             verb would then have to choose first, all or error — e on
             three files, o on three extensions, a on whose directory —
             and this would stop meaning this. the word for a set is
             these, and the selection is already it.
             the seam if it is ever wanted:
             `fm/4-main/13-spawn.go:/m.cur()/` is why the selection
             reaches the tree-writes and not the read verbs. targets()
             is one line there — and then e holds three paths and ed
             opens one, so the verb end owes the same answer.
